WEBVTT

00:00:00.035 --> 00:00:02.694
Choose a small idea you care about, or continue the

00:00:02.752 --> 00:00:04.098
fictional inquiry tracker.

00:00:04.481 --> 00:00:06.920
The goal is not to produce a polished app in a single

00:00:06.954 --> 00:00:07.384
sitting.

00:00:07.767 --> 00:00:10.681
It is to demonstrate that you can lead the process from an

00:00:10.797 --> 00:00:14.826
unstructured idea to a bounded, testable plan and explain

00:00:14.896 --> 00:00:16.730
how execution would be verified.

00:00:17.334 --> 00:00:20.933
Use synthetic examples and avoid sending real messages or

00:00:21.003 --> 00:00:23.511
changing outside systems as part of this exercise.

00:00:23.869 --> 00:00:25.193
Write a brief brain dump.

00:00:25.518 --> 00:00:29.210
Include the problem, people, desired experience,

00:00:29.651 --> 00:00:31.207
constraints, and uncertainty.

00:00:31.904 --> 00:00:34.887
Ask an agent for a structured readback and correct it.

00:00:35.328 --> 00:00:38.707
Your final journey must name an actor, trigger, actions,

00:00:38.730 --> 00:00:39.462
and result.

00:00:40.159 --> 00:00:42.806
Record what you agreed and what is still unknown.

00:00:43.189 --> 00:00:45.882
If the agent proposes technology before understanding the

00:00:45.929 --> 00:00:48.762
desired behavior, bring the conversation back to the

00:00:48.797 --> 00:00:49.307
readback.

00:00:49.749 --> 00:00:52.349
If the behavior is already agreed, proceed from that

00:00:52.419 --> 00:00:52.779
point.

00:00:53.125 --> 00:00:57.223
Create a small reality scan: what exists, what is

00:00:57.293 --> 00:01:00.753
verified, what is merely planned, and what you cannot yet

00:01:00.799 --> 00:01:01.205
know.

00:01:01.902 --> 00:01:04.003
Cite actual evidence where available.

00:01:04.607 --> 00:01:08.450
List two material decisions, compare options, and record

00:01:08.508 --> 00:01:11.132
choices with reasons and reopen conditions.

00:01:11.573 --> 00:01:14.150
For the tracker, you might choose to support the existing

00:01:14.208 --> 00:01:16.147
form first and defer email import.

00:01:16.588 --> 00:01:19.503
Do not mark a real integration live based only on a

00:01:19.561 --> 00:01:22.115
planned diagram or a simulated response.

00:01:22.431 --> 00:01:25.728
Draw a component diagram and trace one successful and one

00:01:25.763 --> 00:01:26.529
failed workflow.

00:01:27.133 --> 00:01:30.279
Identify the source of truth, an access boundary, and at

00:01:30.349 --> 00:01:32.032
least one recovery concern.

00:01:32.636 --> 00:01:36.874
Write a build specification with scope, non-goals, input

00:01:36.920 --> 00:01:41.240
validation, expected outputs, state changes, errors,

00:01:41.414 --> 00:01:45.628
security, preservation requirements, acceptance checks,

00:01:45.872 --> 00:01:46.720
and rollback.

00:01:47.323 --> 00:01:50.226
Translate broad claims into observable behaviors.

00:01:50.609 --> 00:01:53.337
If the owner says the tracker must never send an automatic

00:01:53.395 --> 00:01:56.159
email, the spec must preserve that boundary.

00:01:56.474 --> 00:01:58.320
Break the work into ordered phases.

00:01:58.645 --> 00:02:01.733
Give parallel work exclusive ownership and define shared

00:02:01.768 --> 00:02:02.859
interfaces first.

00:02:03.300 --> 00:02:06.157
Write a kickoff with the project location or placeholder,

00:02:06.598 --> 00:02:10.951
baseline, read order, authority, decisions,

00:02:11.335 --> 00:02:15.781
sequence, verification, usage policy, and handoff.

00:02:16.385 --> 00:02:18.927
Explain how the agent should proceed if a model is

00:02:18.985 --> 00:02:21.354
unavailable or usage is unknown.

00:02:21.737 --> 00:02:24.639
Include a checkpoint and stop conditions for a long run.

00:02:25.336 --> 00:02:28.180
Planning does not by itself authorize deployment or

00:02:28.331 --> 00:02:29.852
unrelated external changes.

00:02:30.157 --> 00:02:32.421
Read the package as someone who has not heard your

00:02:32.468 --> 00:02:33.292
conversation.

00:02:33.675 --> 00:02:36.125
Can they tell which documents govern a behavior?

00:02:36.450 --> 00:02:38.493
Can they identify the first safe action?

00:02:38.877 --> 00:02:41.616
Is each important claim paired with appropriate evidence?

00:02:42.058 --> 00:02:44.368
Does the plan say what stays unchanged?

00:02:44.809 --> 00:02:47.224
Are failures and uncertain effects recoverable?

00:02:47.665 --> 00:02:49.140
Use the rubric below.

00:02:49.465 --> 00:02:52.089
Revise the package where a fresh reader must guess.

00:02:52.530 --> 00:02:56.106
A successful capstone is clear, bounded, testable,

00:02:56.315 --> 00:02:59.612
authorized at the appropriate stage, and restartable.

00:02:59.951 --> 00:03:02.122
Pick a project small enough to describe in a few

00:03:02.168 --> 00:03:02.842
sentences.

00:03:03.167 --> 00:03:06.034
The inquiry tracker is a good example because it has a

00:03:06.127 --> 00:03:10.144
public input, a private owner, a record, and meaningful

00:03:10.214 --> 00:03:11.154
failure cases.

00:03:11.851 --> 00:03:15.079
If you choose your own idea, write the real outcome and

00:03:15.137 --> 00:03:18.307
explicitly exclude at least one tempting future feature.

00:03:18.817 --> 00:03:21.163
You can work with an agent throughout the exercise.

00:03:21.604 --> 00:03:23.740
The goal is to create a package that preserves your

00:03:23.786 --> 00:03:27.490
intentions and gives another agent an actionable, testable

00:03:27.525 --> 00:03:28.198
starting point.

00:03:28.578 --> 00:03:31.388
Give your raw idea to the agent and ask for a structured

00:03:31.422 --> 00:03:31.840
mirror.

00:03:32.224 --> 00:03:34.255
Compare its description to what you intended.

00:03:34.638 --> 00:03:37.831
Check the people involved, the trigger, the actions, and

00:03:37.866 --> 00:03:38.667
the final state.

00:03:39.050 --> 00:03:41.047
Correct an assumption or ambiguity.

00:03:41.430 --> 00:03:44.344
Record the final agreed statement and any unresolved

00:03:44.379 --> 00:03:44.994
questions.

00:03:45.435 --> 00:03:47.908
A fresh reader should be able to distinguish what you

00:03:47.990 --> 00:03:50.509
actually said from what the agent suggested.

00:03:50.892 --> 00:03:53.887
If a decision is already settled, include its source

00:03:53.969 --> 00:03:56.999
rather than forcing an unnecessary repeat discussion.

00:03:57.289 --> 00:03:59.146
Inspect your architecture diagram.

00:03:59.530 --> 00:04:01.561
Does each component have a clear job?

00:04:01.944 --> 00:04:04.510
Can you trace a valid request from beginning to end?

00:04:05.021 --> 00:04:06.658
What is the authoritative record?

00:04:06.983 --> 00:04:09.050
Where do untrusted inputs enter?

00:04:09.375 --> 00:04:11.732
Who may view or change private data?

00:04:12.173 --> 00:04:14.925
Now trace a failure, such as a storage outage.

00:04:15.308 --> 00:04:18.709
A diagram can be simple, but the explanation should make

00:04:18.744 --> 00:04:20.811
responsibilities and recovery visible.

00:04:21.252 --> 00:04:24.584
If the diagram requires facts you have not checked, label

00:04:24.654 --> 00:04:25.757
them as assumptions.

00:04:26.023 --> 00:04:28.449
Choose three requirements and ask a fresh reader to

00:04:28.507 --> 00:04:30.075
describe how each would be proven.

00:04:30.516 --> 00:04:32.919
Add acceptance criteria where answers differ.

00:04:33.302 --> 00:04:36.983
Include a success path, invalid data, and at least one

00:04:37.029 --> 00:04:38.782
failure or authorization case.

00:04:39.223 --> 00:04:40.825
List what the build must preserve.

00:04:41.208 --> 00:04:44.134
Describe a safe order of work and a proposed rollback.

00:04:44.645 --> 00:04:47.060
If implementation needs a provider or an owner's

00:04:47.095 --> 00:04:50.415
permission, state that boundary explicitly rather than

00:04:50.473 --> 00:04:52.145
silently assume it will be available.

00:04:52.513 --> 00:04:55.067
Your final kickoff and handoff should let a new agent find

00:04:55.090 --> 00:04:57.703
the governing documents, identify the revision or

00:04:57.761 --> 00:05:01.081
baseline, know what is allowed, and take the first safe

00:05:01.128 --> 00:05:01.650
action.

00:05:02.091 --> 00:05:05.342
Ask the fresh reader to identify any contradiction or

00:05:05.400 --> 00:05:06.422
missing dependency.

00:05:07.025 --> 00:05:10.810
A useful handoff also distinguishes built, offline-tested,

00:05:11.193 --> 00:05:14.583
provider-accepted, live-observed, and pending.

00:05:15.280 --> 00:05:17.939
If your capstone is only a plan, say so.

00:05:18.380 --> 00:05:20.516
That is a valid outcome for this exercise.

00:05:20.823 --> 00:05:23.772
Score each rubric item and write down any place the reader

00:05:23.830 --> 00:05:24.271
guessed.

00:05:24.712 --> 00:05:27.568
Revise your package and ask the reader to explain the

00:05:27.626 --> 00:05:28.845
revised section again.

00:05:29.356 --> 00:05:33.095
A simulated agent review can find confusing language, but

00:05:33.141 --> 00:05:35.533
it is not the same as observing a real beginner.

00:05:36.229 --> 00:05:39.260
Keep the useful lessons for your next project: a better

00:05:39.318 --> 00:05:42.661
prompt, a clearer template, or a failure pattern worth

00:05:42.719 --> 00:05:43.416
documenting.

00:05:43.927 --> 00:05:46.527
You have now practiced the entire method from cloud to

00:05:46.609 --> 00:05:49.918
contract and have a foundation for authorizing a safe

00:05:49.952 --> 00:05:50.347
build.

00:05:50.651 --> 00:05:53.739
Now imagine your project grows or you hand it to another

00:05:53.786 --> 00:05:54.239
team.

00:05:54.749 --> 00:05:56.828
Which decisions should remain stable?

00:05:57.269 --> 00:05:59.672
Which assumptions are tied to the current environment?

00:06:00.183 --> 00:06:02.923
Which costs or permissions need fresh confirmation?

00:06:03.527 --> 00:06:06.360
Write a short note that answers these questions and points

00:06:06.383 --> 00:06:07.602
to the relevant artifacts.

00:06:08.043 --> 00:06:11.038
This is how the workflow scales beyond a single prompt:

00:06:11.642 --> 00:06:15.555
understanding, decisions, evidence, and the next action

00:06:15.624 --> 00:06:18.991
remain available even when the original conversation is no

00:06:19.072 --> 00:06:20.164
longer in context.
